{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"shady-blueblack-whale","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Client report\nWhile one rebar gate invocation was running, a sibling agent executed `uv tool install --force`. uv garbage-collected the release directory backing the active process, and the gate later crashed with `ImportError` / `FileNotFoundError`. Expected behavior: an invocation resolves one installed release and keeps all code/resources it needs reachable for its entire lifetime, even when another process force-reinstalls the tool.\n\nThe reported explanation is a hypothesis until reproduced and traced. Investigation must distinguish uv installation semantics, Python import timing, subprocess/module resource lookup, and any rebar-controlled launcher or snapshot seam.\n\n## Plan\n- Reproduce with two synchronized real processes: pause an invocation after startup but before a late import/resource read, force-reinstall from the sibling process, then release the gate.\n- Trace the exact path removed and the first late read/import that reaches it; compare against a no-reinstall control and uv upstream behavior.\n- Establish the authoritative lifecycle contract and bounded caller/deployment impact before repair.\n- After confirmed RCA, create a held-out parent/child subprocess RED oracle and implement lifetime pinning at the narrowest owned seam; prove GREEN and exact-defect mutation RED.\n- Sweep CLI, library, MCP, review-bot, and spawned gate-worker entry paths for parity.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A deterministic synchronized-process reproduction proves the changeable root cause and rules out unrelated import/cache races.\n- [ ] A cited contract defines which release/resources remain valid for one invocation.\n- [ ] A real parent/child subprocess test is observed RED before repair, GREEN afterward, and RED under exact-defect mutation.\n- [ ] A force reinstall during an active gate cannot cause that invocation to raise `ImportError` or `FileNotFoundError` from its resolved release.\n- [ ] The next invocation uses the newly installed release; lifetime pinning does not permanently retain obsolete releases.\n- [ ] CLI, library, MCP, automated gate callers, and child-process parity are dispositioned.\n- [ ] Cleanup behavior and operator setup documentation are updated where the proven mechanism requires it.","id":"559b-b7cd-6552-437d","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["client-report","concurrency","installation","gates"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Concurrent uv tool reinstall can delete the active rebar release during a gate invocation"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787067269645569001,"uuid":"40814967-8db2-4fa5-a6f1-e41536edf92f"}